```js
function avoid(car, colliders) {
  const ahead = car.pos.clone().addScaledVector(car.forward, 9);
  const probe = (x,z) => colliders.some(c => x>c.minX-1.5 && x<c.maxX+1.5 && z>c.minZ-1.5 && z<c.maxZ+1.5);
  if (probe(ahead.x, ahead.z)) {
    const left = car.pos.clone().addScaledVector(car.forward, 8).addScaledVector(car.right, -6);
    const rightP = ... +6;
    if (!probe(left.x,left.z)) steer += 0.8; else if (!probe(rightP...)) steer -= 0.8; else steer = 1;
  }
}
```
Wait steering positive turns west/left? With forward north, positive heading turns west = left. So if obstacle ahead, prefer side free. Fine.

Cops should follow roads? Direct chase through buildings with avoidance can get stuck on block corners. Building blocks are large; chasing directly across blocks impossible. Better: use road-aware navigation. Since city grid roads, compute target waypoint on road network toward player: if player and cop are on different roads, navigate via intersections. Simple approach: choose the nearest intersection to player, and nearest intersection to cop; if not aligned on same road, steer to an intermediate intersection.

Implement waypoint navigation:
- Represent intersection grid points (x,z) from ROAD_LINES.
- Cop's current nearest road: determine which road line is closest to cop x and z. If cop is on a vertical road (|cop.x - roadX| < 9) then it can move along z toward the player's z road. If not, steer to nearest intersection.
- Simpler: compute target intersection: 
  - Let px, pz = player.pos. Find nearest road index for player x (rx) and z (rz).
  - For cop, find nearest road x (cxr) and z (czr).
  - If cop is on a vertical road (distance to cxr < 10) and the player's z road differs, target = (cxr, playerZroad?) Hmm.

Maybe direct chase + avoidance is enough in a grid city if we make cops drive on roads by targeting the player's *road-aligned* position. Actually since player mostly drives on roads, direct line-of-sight chase often follows roads. When player turns a corner, cop may cut through block and get stuck; avoidance pushes along building edge, often sliding around. Acceptable for arcade.

Improve with simple "road snapping" target: 
- Find whether cop is closer to a vertical or horizontal road: dxRoad = distance to nearest ROAD_LINES x, dzRoad = distance to nearest ROAD_LINES z.
- If on vertical road (dxRoad < 8): steer toward (cop.x snapped? use player's x road if far?) target z = player.z, x = cop road x. If the player is far in x (> 30), then target the intersection at (cop road x, nearest player z road) to turn. 
- If on horizontal road: analogous.
- If not on a road: steer toward nearest intersection.
This way cops follow the road network like taxis. Let's implement:

```js
function navTarget(car, playerPos) {
  const rx = nearestRoad(car.pos.x);
  const rz = nearestRoad(car.pos.z);
  const dxr = Math.abs(car.pos.x - rx);
  const dzr = Math.abs(car.pos.z - rz);
  const onV = dxr < 9;
  const onH = dzr < 9;
  if (onV && onH) {
    // at intersection: aim directly at player (will pick a road)
    return playerPos;
  }
  if (onV && !onH) {
    // along z on vertical road: target x=rx, z=player.z; if player x is far, first go to intersection
    const pxRoad = nearestRoad(playerPos.x);
    if (Math.abs(playerPos.x - rx) > 24) {
      // go to next intersection toward player z then turn
      return new THREE.Vector3(rx, 0, nearestRoad(playerPos.z));
    }
    return new THREE.Vector3(rx, 0, playerPos.z);
  }
  if (onH && !onV) {
    const pzRoad = nearestRoad(playerPos.z);
    if (Math.abs(playerPos.z - rz) > 24) {
      return new THREE.Vector3(nearestRoad(playerPos.x), 0, rz);
    }
    return new THREE.Vector3(playerPos.x, 0, rz);
  }
  // off-road: nearest intersection
  return new THREE.Vector3(rx, 0, rz);
}
```
This makes cops drive on roads. When near player (<25m) and line of sight, direct chase. Add line of sight check? Direct if distance < 30.

Cop heading needs to reach waypoint; if within 6m of waypoint, target player.

